TASK: I/O-Optimized Deployment Planning + Execution from a WIDGET Document Suite (Darshan IODD)

ROLE
You are an autonomous deployment + execution agent. Your ONLY knowledge of the target
workflow comes from a WIDGET v7 six-document YAML suite. From it you must (1) produce an
I/O-optimized deployment plan and (2) actually run the workflow on the cluster and record
its runtime. You must also record the cost (tokens / context / time / tool calls) you spend
in each phase.

=========================================================================================
EXPERIMENTAL CONTROL — KNOWLEDGE BOUNDARY (read carefully)
=========================================================================================
- The six YAML files in
    /u/mtang9/widget_eval/widget-v1/workflow-representation-description/generated_suites/montage_25n_darshan/
  are your SOLE source of truth about what the workflow is, how it currently deploys, the
  hardware, the goals, and its observed I/O behavior.
- Do NOT read the workflow's source code, the raw profiler traces, README files, design
  docs, or any campaign/notes files to UNDERSTAND the workflow. Deriving workflow knowledge
  from anything other than the six YAMLs invalidates the experiment.
- You MAY use the EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT paths below purely to LAUNCH the run (binaries,
  input data, scheduler). Treat them as opaque execution substrate, not as a knowledge
  source. Do not open the Montage source to learn behavior.
- The observed-I/O document (iodd.yaml) in this suite was produced by the **Darshan**
  profiler. Base every I/O optimization on what THIS IODD records. Where the IODD marks a
  field "not captured by darshan", you do NOT have that information — do not substitute
  outside knowledge to fill the gap. If a gap blocks an optimization, report it as a gap.

=========================================================================================
INPUT — the document suite (read all six)
=========================================================================================
Directory: /u/mtang9/widget_eval/widget-v1/workflow-representation-description/generated_suites/montage_25n_darshan/
- wdd.yaml  — logical structure (stages, tasks, data_objects, pc_edges, patterns, io_behavioral_hints)
- edd.yaml  — experiment instance (scale, input data path, resolved parameters)
- gd.yaml   — goals / SLOs / hard constraints
- hrd.yaml  — hardware (compute classes, storage tiers, interconnect)
- ddd.yaml  — current deployment strategy + scheduler directives
- iodd.yaml — observed I/O profile from Darshan (per-task bytes/ops, bottlenecks, optimization_opportunities)

=========================================================================================
EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT (substrate only — for launching, not for learning the workflow)
=========================================================================================
- Cluster: NCSA Delta (as described in hrd.yaml). Account / partition / node-count: take
  from ddd.yaml scheduler directives (do not invent your own).
- Montage binaries: /u/mtang9/widget_eval/hpc_workflows/repos/Montage/bin   (prepend to PATH)
- Workflow input data: the path resolved in edd.yaml (Scale-A synthetic FITS + region.hdr).
- Run dir root: /projects/bekn/mtang9/widget/runs/Montage/  — create a NEW subdir for your
  run (e.g. opt_darshan_<timestamp>). NEVER overwrite mn25_datalife / mn25_darshan or any
  data dir.
- Results dir (you create): .../generated_suites/montage_25n_darshan/agent_run_results/
- Optional table sharder: /u/mtang9/widget_eval/hpc_workflows/scripts/tbl_tool.py (split/concat .tbl only).
- SLURM cluster; --account is REQUIRED on every job. Submit via sbatch.
- If Jarvis-MCP tools (jm_*, create_pipeline, append_pkg, build_pipeline_env, run_pipeline)
  are available in your session, you may deploy via Jarvis instead of a raw SLURM script.

=========================================================================================
PHASE 0 — METRICS BOOTSTRAP (do this first, before reading the YAMLs in full)
=========================================================================================
- Run: for f in <suite_dir>/*.yaml; do wc -c "$f"; done  → record bytes per file.
- Compute est_tokens = ceil(bytes / 4) per file and the suite total. This is the
  deterministic "ingestion cost" of the widget suite.
- Print, alone on a line, the sentinel:  @@WIDGET_METRIC planning_start@@
- Record planning_start = `date -u +%s` (and ISO time).

=========================================================================================
PHASE 1 — I/O-OPTIMIZED DEPLOYMENT PLAN  (the "planning" phase)
=========================================================================================
Using ONLY the six YAMLs:
1. Summarize the workflow from wdd.yaml: stages, tasks, DAG/topological order, P-C patterns,
   primary workflow pattern, and any io_behavioral_hints.
2. From ddd.yaml, restate the CURRENT deployment: per-task parallelism, storage-tier
   assignments, and scheduler directives.
3. From iodd.yaml (DataLife), extract the observed I/O facts you will optimize against:
   per-task bytes read/written and op counts, request-size / data-flow detail where present,
   bottleneck_summary, anomalies, and optimization_opportunities. QUOTE the specific IODD
   fields you rely on (so the basis of each decision is auditable).
4. From hrd.yaml, identify which storage tiers / hardware features are actually available to
   exploit (faster tier, node-local, stripe tuning, etc.). Use only what the HRD lists.
5. From gd.yaml, list the goals and HARD constraints your plan must respect (node budget,
   must-produce outputs, output-location constraints, priority ranking).
6. PRODUCE the I/O-optimized deployment plan: concrete, justified changes relative to the
   current DDD (e.g. storage-tier reassignment, node-local staging, parallelism changes,
   stripe tuning, request-size/aggregation changes, stage overlap). For EACH proposed change
   state: (a) the IODD evidence motivating it, (b) the GD goal it serves, (c) confirmation it
   honors every hard constraint. If an optimization you would normally make is blocked
   because Darshan did not capture the needed evidence, SAY SO explicitly — that gap is a
   result, not a failure.
7. Write the plan to <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/deployment_plan.md.
8. Record planning_end = `date -u +%s`; then print, alone on a line:  @@WIDGET_METRIC planning_end@@

=========================================================================================
PHASE 2 — RUN THE WORKFLOW + RECORD RUNTIME  (the "running" phase)
=========================================================================================
Print, alone on a line, the sentinel:  @@WIDGET_METRIC running_start@@   then:
1. Translate your plan into an executable deployment. The ddd.yaml scheduler_directives +
   per-task placement, plus the WDD executables and pc_edges, give you everything needed to
   construct the run; apply your optimizations on top. (The DDD encodes a Class-A srun
   fan-out: one task per node for the scatter/*Exec stages, single-task for the
   mImgtbl/mOverlaps/mBgModel/mAdd stages.) Prefer Jarvis-MCP if available; else build and
   sbatch a SLURM script.
2. Create a fresh run dir under the run-dir root. Apply any tier/stripe/staging optimization
   to that dir BEFORE launching (e.g. `lfs setstripe ...` if your plan calls for it).
3. Submit, then wait for completion. Record SEPARATELY:
   - queue_wait_seconds (submit → start), and
   - workflow_runtime_seconds (actual pipeline elapsed — from the run's own timing output,
     cross-checked with `sacct -j <jobid> --format=JobID,Elapsed,NNodes,State`).
   Verify the run produced the workflow's terminal output and used the intended node count.
4. Write <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/run_report.md: what you ran, which optimizations were
   applied, success/failure, workflow_runtime_seconds, node count, and any deviation from plan.
5. Record running metrics (see METRICS PROTOCOL); then print, alone on a line:  @@WIDGET_METRIC running_end@@

=========================================================================================
METRICS PROTOCOL (mandatory) — authoritative tokens WITHOUT API access
=========================================================================================
Token spend is measured authoritatively by parsing THIS session's local Claude Code
transcript (~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/<session-uuid>.jsonl), which records the real
input/output/cache token counts per turn. You do NOT estimate tokens. Your job is to make
the run parseable and to record the metrics only you know (wall time, tool counts, file
sizes, workflow runtime).

1. PHASE SENTINELS — you already print these four lines EXACTLY, alone on a line, at the
   phase boundaries; they delimit the transcript so tokens attribute per phase:
     @@WIDGET_METRIC planning_start@@   (first action, before reading anything)
     @@WIDGET_METRIC planning_end@@     (right after writing deployment_plan.md)
     @@WIDGET_METRIC running_start@@    (right before constructing/launching the run)
     @@WIDGET_METRIC running_end@@      (right after the run completes + run_report.md)

2. Write <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/metrics.json with the NON-token metrics you measure:
{
  "agent": "<your agent/model name and version>",
  "profiler_suite": "darshan",
  "session_transcript_hint": "<your session uuid, or 'newest jsonl under the project'>",
  "context_ingestion": {
    "yaml_files": [{"file":"wdd.yaml","bytes":N,"est_tokens":N}, ... all six ...],
    "total_bytes": N, "total_est_tokens": N
  },
  "planning": { "wall_seconds": N, "tool_calls": N, "files_read": [{"path":"...","bytes":N}] },
  "running":  { "wall_seconds": N, "queue_wait_seconds": N, "workflow_runtime_seconds": N, "tool_calls": N }
}
  (est_tokens = ceil(bytes/4): the deterministic "cost of reading the yaml files", which
  complements the authoritative transcript token counts.)

3. AUTHORITATIVE TOKENS — after the run, the real per-phase token + tool-call counts come from:
     python3 .../workflow-representation-description/measure_agent_cost.py --latest \
       --out <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/metrics_tokens.json
   It segments the transcript by your sentinels and reports real input/output/cache tokens
   per phase (tokens_are_estimate=false). Run it yourself at the very end if you can; either
   way LEAVE THE SENTINELS so it can be run post-hoc. Do not fabricate token numbers.

4. Use `date -u +%s` for wall timing. Count every tool call per phase.

5. At the very end of your final message, print the planning and running summaries inline in
   two fenced blocks labeled `METRICS:PLANNING` and `METRICS:RUNNING` (wall time, tool calls,
   workflow_runtime_seconds, and — if you ran the parser — the authoritative token counts).

=========================================================================================
HARD SCOPE RULES
=========================================================================================
- The six YAMLs are the ONLY workflow-knowledge source. No source code, raw traces, or
  notes/campaign files for understanding the workflow.
- Respect every gd.yaml hard constraint; stay within the node budget.
- Never overwrite the existing trace run dirs (mn25_datalife, mn25_darshan) or any data dir.
- Before any destructive action, print exactly what will change.
- If a required value is absent from the suite, use an explicit placeholder and report it as
  missing — do not fetch it from outside.

=========================================================================================
OUTPUT
=========================================================================================
1. <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/deployment_plan.md
2. <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/run_report.md
3. <suite_dir>/agent_run_results/metrics.json
4. A final message summarizing: the optimization(s) chosen, the achieved
   workflow_runtime_seconds, and the two inline METRICS blocks.

(<suite_dir> = /u/mtang9/widget_eval/widget-v1/workflow-representation-description/generated_suites/montage_25n_darshan)
